<LabLayout title="CardioRotovator model & assumptions" description="Synchronized CardioRotovator geometry and separate finite-mass passive dynamics: equations, assumptions and numerical checks.">
<main class="guide" id="lab-content"><header class="guide-header"><p class="micro">SYNCHRONIZED GEOMETRY / PASSIVE DYNAMICS</p><h1>A long arm needs the right phase.</h1><p>The intended synchronized path and the uncontrolled rigid-body experiment answer different questions.</p><a class="button-link primary" href="/lab/cardio/">Open CardioRotovator →</a></header>
<section class="guide-section"><h2>The reference concept</h2><p>HASTOL describes a single tapered arm phased inward at apogee and outward at perigee, with two rotations per orbit. Its analysis identifies pickup-induced orbital change and the need for active resynchronization as major difficulties. See <a href="https://www.niac.usra.edu/files/studies/final_report/355Bogar.pdf#page=21">HASTOL Phase I, pp. 17–19, Figure 13</a>.</p><p>The main view demonstrates the prescribed geometry. The separate uncontrolled-dynamics section tests a freely moving rigid body. Setting its initial spin to two turns per orbit does not make that free body stay synchronized.</p></section>
<section class="guide-section"><h2>The synchronized reference</h2><p>The reference uses a Kepler ellipse for the station and exactly two inertial tether rotations per station orbit. Time zero is station apogee, with the arm pointing inward. At half a period the station is at perigee and the arm points outward; after a full period both return to their original geometry. This relation is prescribed, not achieved by a simulated controller.</p><p>For station radii rₐ and rₚ, a = (rₐ + rₚ)/2 and T = 2π√(a³/μ). Arm length L is the station apogee altitude minus the selected pickup altitude. Its inertial angle is θ = π + 4πt/T; the grapple position is station position + L(cos θ, sin θ). Tip velocity includes both station orbital velocity and the rotating-arm contribution. The displayed tip path follows those equations rather than a drawn heart-shaped template. The relation L = rₐ − (Earth radius + pickup altitude) and rotational period T/2 also appear in HASTOL Appendix A1, p. A1-9.</p><p>Station and tip paths share one physical scale. The closest point on the entire arm determines sampled geometric clearance. A low pickup altitude is a location in this geometric construction; drag, heating, capture guidance, tether mass and control/reboost requirements are not solved here. The reference does not provide payload capacity, structural qualification or campaign performance.</p></section>
<section class="guide-section"><h2>Trace a release from the reference</h2><p>Pause at a phase and choose “Trace release here.” The C1k-0.1.0 diagnostic starts an ideal test particle at the exact reference tip position and velocity, with no release kick. It propagates only that particle under spherical Earth gravity for up to 30 minutes. The drawing holds the tether at its release configuration and fits the future particle path at the same scale.</p><p>Point-particle RK4 uses two-second steps. A detected 120 km crossing is bracketed within the step; the drawn path stops just above that boundary. A tip already below 120 km has no propagated coast. Orbit elements classify the future path independently of the short drawing: an Earth-bound orbit returns to perigee, while an outward escape does not return to a perigee in its past. A crossing beyond the drawn segment is still flagged. For escape, the excess speed far from Earth is √(2ε), where ε is specific orbital energy.</p><p>The particle has no effect on the reference tether: payload mass, recoil, loads, synchronization effort, reboost and destination targeting are excluded. An escape trajectory alone does not establish a safe or repeatable delivery. Changing geometry or replay phase clears the trace. Exports retain the exact reference inputs, release state, coast samples, outcome and numerical residuals; they do not change saved passive designs.</p></section>
<section class="guide-section"><h2>Compare reference phases</h2><p>The comparison samples 21 release states at 5% steps through one reference orbit. Each uses the same tip state and future-orbit classification as “Trace release here.” The samples classify orbit elements without integrating every coast; selecting a sample then calculates its 30-minute trace. Both 0% and 100% represent apogee.</p><p>These discrete results do not establish continuous safe intervals. The intervening phases, tether loads and synchronization effort are untested. Opening the comparison preserves the current trace; selecting a phase replaces it. Geometry edits update the samples, and invalid inputs hide their actions. The comparison export records the exact geometry, model, phase, initial state and orbital result for every sample, independently of display units.</p></section>
<section class="guide-section"><h2>Uncontrolled dynamics: C1p-0.1.1</h2><p>A finite station, linearly tapered rigid arm and 2 t grapple move in planar spherical-Earth gravity. The station-to-tip coordinate is s; the body’s mass-weighted center is c. For center-of-mass position R, velocity V, angle θ and spin ω, each material point has position R + (s − c)e and velocity V + (s − c)ωe⊥.</p><p>Sum gravitational force at every mass element to accelerate the center of mass. Sum torques about that center to obtain angular acceleration. In this passive experiment, no prescribed station orbit, constant-spin motor or phase-lock controller drives the solid replay paths. The optional dashed curve instead uses a Kepler center-of-mass orbit and constant spin, solely as a geometric reference.</p><p>The initial center-of-mass apogee and perigee define the osculating ellipse and nominal period T. Initial angular speed is the selected ratio × 2π/T; zero phase offset points the working arm inward at apogee. This initial ratio is not maintained. Unwrapped spin drift measures θ − (θ₀ + ω₀t), so it includes any lost or gained full turns.</p></section>
<section class="guide-section"><h2>Ideal pickup and two coasts</h2><p>The incoming payload starts at exactly the tip position and velocity. Adding it changes the combined center of mass, its translational velocity and inertia. The state is reframed so every existing material point retains its position and velocity. Combined energy and angular momentum include the arriving payload and close across the attachment.</p><p>The empty and loaded facilities then coast independently for at most one original nominal orbit. The payload remains attached; there is no release. The table’s initial perigee is the point-orbit estimate for each center-of-mass state, not a cable-clearance guarantee. A completed coast means that this sampled run did not reach a stop limit; it does not establish a repeatable pickup.</p></section>
<section class="guide-section"><h2>Mass, load and clearance</h2><p>The independent passive scenario uses an 800 km arm, a 5,000 t station, 10 t incoming payload, 500 mm² tip cable area and a 3:1 station-to-tip area ratio. Initial COM altitudes are 1,200 km perigee and 1,800 km apogee. These are experiment settings. Zylon HM density and strength use the existing Lab material profile, with safety factor two; the linear taper is not optimized.</p><p>Forty-eight cable cells use two-point Gauss quadrature. This integrates the linear taper’s mass, first moment and rotational inertia exactly; gravity and potential are approximated at the same nodes. Axial cut forces sum the outboard inertial-minus-gravitational forces, including the terminal load at the exact tip cross-section. Transverse reaction is reported separately because a rigid line can require forces a flexible cable cannot provide.</p><p>Clearance uses the closest point on the entire straight arm segment. Each coast stops below 120 km, above the axial allowable or when a cut requires more than 100 N of compression. Classical RK4 steps are at most five seconds and 0.01 rad of rotation; a detected limit is bracketed within the step. Checks are numerical samples, not a certified continuous clearance bound. Energy/angular-momentum drift, capture closure and step/quadrature convergence tests are available in the report and source.</p></section>
<section class="guide-section"><h2>Choose a release time</h2><p>The separate passive C1r-0.1.1 experiment starts from the same ideal matched pickup. Select 5–90% of the original nominal period. The integrator steps exactly to that instant, records the loaded state, removes the payload and reframes the remaining center of mass. Every tether material point keeps its position and velocity; cargo inherits the tip’s inertial position and velocity. No release impulse, separation motor or recovery energy is added.</p><p>The unloaded rigid tether and free cargo then propagate under central Earth gravity until the end of that original period or the first tether/cargo limit. Cargo uses point-mass RK4. Energy and angular momentum are summed across both objects; separation closure and total drift appear in the report. Mutual tether–cargo gravity, collision detection, atmosphere and release hardware are not modeled.</p><p>Cargo perigee and apogee are calculated from its release state. A coast can end before the cargo reaches an unsafe perigee, so the interface flags a future 120 km crossing even when the finite replay completes. A bound orbit will return to perigee; inbound unbound cargo will reach its perigee. An already outbound unbound trajectory may have its low perigee in the past, so it is not rejected for a future crossing that will not occur. Negative perigee altitude means the mathematical orbit intersects Earth; no trajectory is propagated below the cutoff. The remaining tether’s center-of-mass perigee is a diagnostic, not a clearance guarantee.</p><p>Replay keeps separate frames immediately before and after separation, with no interpolation across the mass change. The release report includes the design, timing and both trajectories. Release timing is a session experiment; the original C1p design save and share formats remain unchanged. This is not a repeatable delivery, destination targeting or reboost model.</p></section>
<section class="guide-section"><h2>Compare release timings</h2><p>The timing comparison runs the same C1r model at 5% increments from 5–90% of the original period, adding your exact selected timing when it falls between those samples. Hardware, orbit, payload and spin stay fixed. Results arrive one at a time; stopping keeps completed samples available.</p><p>A clear passive sample must complete its calculated coast without a future cargo crossing of the 120 km boundary. A tether failure still fails that sample even when cargo alone clears the boundary. Selecting a card opens the exact calculated flight and focuses its verdict. The gaps between samples remain untested; the comparison does not establish a continuous safe release window or repeatable service.</p><p>Timing comparisons stay in this session. Exported summaries include the exact design and timing plan, completed rows and a flag distinguishing a complete run from a partial one. The release report remains available for the full trajectory of an inspected sample.</p></section>
<section class="guide-section"><h2>What remains outside the model</h2><p>No atmospheric ascent, incoming guidance, grapple dynamics, capture shock, flexible cable, bending qualification, reeling, motor control, reboost, orbit perturbations or repeatable traffic service is solved. An ideal matched pickup is supplied, not discovered. The rigid-body result is useful for comparing phase and orbital sensitivity within those assumptions.</p><p>Passive designs have their own browser save key and versioned JSON. C1p-0.1.0 inputs remain readable with their numeric settings preserved; calculations now use C1p-0.1.1, which includes the terminal stress correction. Loading alone does not rewrite a stored design. The synchronized reference controls are separate session settings. They do not overwrite Earth, Moon, Phobos, T4 or Expeditions saves. This experiment cannot be commissioned into Expeditions; the Earth bridge still requires its separate two-delivery model.</p></section>
